.TH VMIDID 8 "2026-10-05" "VLHE 1.0.1" "VLHE Manual"
.SH NAME
vmidid \- play VLHE's MIDI device through SoundFont 2 banks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vmidid
.B \-s
.I font.sf2
.RB [ \-s
.IR font.sf2 [@ bank ]]...
.RB [ \-d
.IR dev ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR dsp ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR voices ]
.RB [ \-A " on|off]"
.RB [ \-a
.IR spec ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR rate ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR gain ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR law ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR mode ]
.RB [ \-E " on|off|reverb|chorus]"
.RB [ \-B " on|off]"
.RB [ \-M " fast|reference]"
.RB [ \-R
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR frames ]
.RB [ \-q
.IR blocks ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B vmidi.o
kernel module is a MIDI device,
.IR /dev/vmidi ,
that appears to programs as an ordinary OSS MIDI port - MIDI device
0 to most of them.
.B vmidid
reads what programs send it and renders the music with SoundFont 2
instruments, playing the result through vsound like any other
program - or, when vsound is not loaded, straight to the sound card -
so a card with no wavetable gets General MIDI.
.PP
A SoundFont is required; it is not part of VLHE. Any General MIDI
.I .sf2
file works. More than one may be stacked: the first
.B \-s
is the base font, in MIDI bank 0, and each later one is searched
before it. The base font is always bank 0 and takes no
.BI @ bank\fR.
.IB font.sf2 @ bank
puts that font's bank 0 in MIDI bank
.IR bank ,
which is how a song that brings its own instruments in bank 1 is
played. Up to eight fonts.
.PP
It is normally started by
.B vlhe apply
or the control centre's Load button, with options from
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
and the SoundFonts the user has chosen (or the machine's, when the
user has chosen none), and runs until it is sent SIGTERM or SIGINT.
It also stops if reading the MIDI device or writing the sound fails.
Only one may run at a time. The control centre's Apply settings
button can change most settings while it runs; a new sample rate
takes effect the next time the synth lets its channel go, after
.B \-R
milliseconds of silence, and never while
.B \-R
is 0.
.PP
On a slow machine the synth can fall behind, and the symptom is
silence or stuttering rather than an error. Lowering the sample rate
helps most, because it gives each block more time; fewer voices, and
turning the effects off, help less.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " font.sf2\fR[\fP@bank\fR]"
A SoundFont to play. Required; repeat to stack more, as above. Only
a later
.B \-s
may carry
.BI @ bank\fR.
.TP
.BI \-d " dev"
The MIDI device read. Default
.IR /dev/vmidi .
.TP
.BI \-o " dsp"
Where the sound goes. Default
.IR /dev/dsp .
VLHE starts it with a tag instead:
.B @VSOUND@
waits until vsound is loaded and its pump is ready and plays through
it;
.BI @VSOUND: path @
plays through vsound if it is ready, and otherwise to
.IR path ,
the sound card.
.TP
.BI \-p " voices"
The most voices that may sound at once, 1 to 512; a larger number is
lowered to 512, and 0 or a negative number is ignored. Default 64.
When it runs out, a voice is stolen: one already released or held
only by the sustain pedal goes first, then the oldest and quietest;
drums are kept longest.
.TP
.BR \-A " on|off"
Automatic voice reduction. Default on. When the synth's own output
buffer is draining, it ends fading notes early and lowers the voice
limit, then puts the limit back when it has caught up. It never goes
above
.BR \-p .
.TP
.BI \-a " spec"
Tune
.BR \-A ,
as
.IR name = value
pairs separated by commas:
.B drain
(default 50),
.B emergency
(10) and
.B healthy
(75), which are buffer fills in per cent and must keep emergency less
than drain less than healthy;
.B settle
(100), milliseconds between actions;
.B floor
(8), the fewest voices it will leave; and
.B tails
(1), 1 to end fading notes before lowering the limit, 0 only to lower
the limit.
.TP
.BI \-r " rate"
The sample rate, 8000 to 46340. Default 44100.
.TP
.BI \-g " gain"
The master gain, 0.001 to 2.0: 1.0 is unity and 0.5 the default.
Whatever is too loud to fit is clipped and counted.
.TP
.BI \-L " law"
The volume curve for note velocity and the volume controllers:
.B spec
(or 0, the default), the SoundFont standard; or
.B linear
(or 1), the gentler curve the OPL3 and original AWE32 drivers used,
which often suits DOS-era games better.
.TP
.BI \-F " mode"
The velocity filter - how hard a note maps to brightness:
.B awe
(or 1, the default),
.B 2.01
(or 2),
.B 2.04
(or 3), or
.B none
(or 0), which is what fluidsynth does. Not the same setting as
.BR \-L .
.TP
.BR \-E " on|off|reverb|chorus"
Reverb and chorus: on (the default) for both, off for neither,
.B reverb
or
.B chorus
for that one alone. Off saves processor time; the reverb is nearly all
of it, so
.B chorus
alone keeps some width for most of the saving. The control command
.B set effects
takes the same words.
.TP
.BR \-B " on|off"
Filter bypass. Default off. On skips the low-pass filter for a voice
whose cutoff is above anything its sample can carry.
.TP
.BR \-M " fast|reference"
The modulation envelope. Default fast: the envelope of a voice that
modulates nothing is skipped and caught up exactly when it starts to,
which saves synth work. Reference steps every voice's pitch and filter
envelope every sample, as the SoundFont specification describes. The two
render identically on everything tested.
.TP
.BI \-R " ms"
Milliseconds of silence - no note sounding and the effects faded -
before the synth lets its sound channel go, so the card can be
released between songs. Default 3000; 0 holds the channel as long as
the synth runs.
.TP
.BI \-i " ms"
Milliseconds without MIDI input before every note is turned off, so a
program that stops mid-note does not leave it hanging. Default 2000; 0
turns this off.
.TP
.BI \-b " frames"
Frames per block, 16 to 8192. Default 256.
.TP
.BI \-q " blocks"
Blocks rendered ahead, 0 to 64. Default 0. Kept for comparison only:
it adds delay and protects nothing. (Not "quiet".)
.TP
.B \-v
Report what was negotiated, and a line every second.
.TP
.B \-V
Report every voice as it starts. Very verbose.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Lower the voice limit by 8.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Raise it by 8.
.IP
Either one turns automatic voice reduction off until the synth is
restarted or the control centre's Apply settings turns it on again,
so a limit set by hand is not moved back.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/vlhe/ctl/
Its process ID file and control channel.
.TP
.IR /usr/share/sounds/sf2 ", " /usr/local/share/sounds/sf2 ", " ~/.vlhe/sf2
Where the control centre looks for SoundFonts.
.B vmidid
itself plays only the files named with
.BR \-s .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped by a signal after running normally, or stopped because the
MIDI device could no longer be read or the sound written.
.TP
.B 1
It could not start: another
.B vmidid
is running, an option was bad or out of range, no SoundFont was
given or it could not be loaded, a device could not be opened or
gave a sample rate outside 8000 to 46340, or there was not enough
memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Play with one General MIDI font, at half rate with 32 voices for a
slow machine:
.PP
.RS
vmidid \-s /usr/local/share/sounds/sf2/gm.sf2 \-r 22050 \-p 32
.RE
.PP
Add a song's own font in bank 1:
.PP
.RS
vmidid \-s gm.sf2 \-s song.sf2@1
.RE
.SH BUGS
The usage message refers to design documents that are not shipped.
.PP
A bank or preset cannot yet be remapped when a font is stacked.
